WEBVTT

NOTE Chapter: 1. The Dictionary Dilemma: Revised Edition

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.190
Imagine searching for a word in an encyclopedia with ten thousand pages.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.960
Binary search divides and conquers the problem with logarithmic speed.

NOTE Chapter: 2. The Invariant: Sorted Arrays & Three Pointers

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:15.480
Binary search has one non-negotiable prerequisite: the array must be sorted.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:18.730
Without order, division is impossible.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:26.280
We maintain three key pointers: Low at the start of our search window, High at the end, and Mid right in the center.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:33.810
The formula is simple: Mid equals Low plus High minus Low divided by two, avoiding integer overflow.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:37.700
In each step, we inspect only the element at Mid.

NOTE Chapter: 3. The Elimination Engine: Step-by-Step Execution

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.800
Let us watch the algorithm in motion.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.320
We are searching for 23.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.600
Mid points to index 4, which holds the value 16.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:55.340
Since 16 is strictly less than 23, our target cannot possibly exist anywhere from index 0 to 4.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.310
We eliminate the entire left half!

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:02.470
We shift Low to Mid plus one, which is index 5.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:06.210
Now our new window is from index 5 to 9.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:10.840
We recalculate Mid to index 7, which holds 56.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.680
56 is greater than 23, so we eliminate the right half!

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:21.310
Finally, Low and High converge on index 5, value 23.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.570
Target found in just three comparisons!

NOTE Chapter: 4. Clean Code Implementation & Variable Watch

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:27.970
Here is the canonical Python implementation.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.000
Notice the while loop condition: while low is less than or equal to high.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.870
That 'less than or equal' is crucial for arrays with an odd length or single elements.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:43.910
Inside the loop, if array at mid matches target, we return mid.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:47.860
If the target is greater, we search the right sub-array.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.660
Otherwise, we search the left.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:55.240
If low crosses high without a match, we return minus one.

NOTE Chapter: 5. Time Complexity: Why O(log N) Changes Everything

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.390
The logarithmic efficiency of binary search is staggering.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:05.600
For an array of 1 million items, linear search would take on average 500,000 checks.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.880
Binary search takes at most 20 checks.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:17.010
For 4 billion items, the entire internet population, binary search finds any user in just 32 operations.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:21.240
It turns an impossible problem into an instantaneous lookup.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:26.730
Remember: sorted input, three pointers, eliminate half each step.
